{"name":"Compass Blue","hex":"#35475b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"compass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A magnetic or electronic device used to determine the cardinal directions (usually magnetic or true north).","A pair of compasses (a device used to draw circular arcs and transfer length measurements)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To surround; to encircle; to environ; to stretch round.","To go about or round entirely; to traverse."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/compass"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Compass","blocks":[{"text":"A compass is a device that shows the cardinal directions used for navigation and geographic orientation. It typically consists of a magnetized needle or another element, such as a compass card or compass rose, that pivots to align itself with magnetic north. Other methods may be used, including gyroscopes, magnetometers, and GPS receivers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/2016_Busola.JPG/330px-2016_Busola.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compass","wikidata":"Q34735"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Model_Si_Nan_of_Han_Dynasty.jpg/500px-Model_Si_Nan_of_Han_Dynasty.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Model_Si_Nan_of_Han_Dynasty.jpg/500px-Model_Si_Nan_of_Han_Dynasty.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Model of a lodestone compass from Han dynasty","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AModel_Si_Nan_of_Han_Dynasty.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Walkers_compass_arp.jpg/500px-Walkers_compass_arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Walkers_compass_arp.jpg/500px-Walkers_compass_arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A liquid-filled protractor or orienteering compass with lanyard","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWalkers_compass_arp.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Cammenga-lensatic-compass-model-27.jpg/500px-Cammenga-lensatic-compass-model-27.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Cammenga-lensatic-compass-model-27.jpg/1280px-Cammenga-lensatic-compass-